{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \b \fs36 The Lighthouse at Marrow Point\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0  Eleanor Vance\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Prologue\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The lamp had not been lit for eleven years when the first letter arrived. It came in a plain brown envelope, addressed in a careful hand to {\i The Keeper}, as though the post office had simply forgotten that there was no longer any such person.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Nobody in the village could say who had carried it up the cliff path. By morning it was lying on the step, weighted down with a smooth grey stone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Chapter One\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mara Ellison had inherited three things from her grandfather: a brass telescope, a talent for silence, and the key to a building that the council wanted demolished. She kept all three in the same drawer and tried not to think about any of them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"You could sell it,\u8221" her brother said, not for the first time. \u8220"The land alone must be worth something.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"It is not for sale, Tomas.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"Everything is for sale. That is the whole point of money.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She did not answer him. Outside the kitchen window the tide was coming in, and the long shadow of the tower lay across the water like the hand of a clock. She had watched that shadow every evening of her childhood and she knew, without looking at her watch, that it was nearly seven.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The letter was waiting when she climbed the path the next morning. She turned it over twice before she opened it, and afterwards she wished that she had left it where it lay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Light the lamp on the ninth,} it said. {\i Someone is coming home.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There was no signature. There was only a small drawing in the corner of the page, a ship with three masts, and she had seen that ship before, carved into the underside of her grandfather\u8217's desk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Chapter Two\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The harbour master was a broad, slow-moving woman named Agnes Thorne who had never once been surprised by anything. She read the letter at arm\u8217's length, the way she read everything, and handed it back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"Well,\u8221" she said. \u8220"That is your grandfather\u8217's ship, right enough. The {\i Corriveau}. Lost with all hands in the winter of sixty-one.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"I know when it was lost.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"Then you know nobody is coming home on it.\u8221" Agnes folded her hands. \u8220"Somebody is having a joke with you, girl. An unkind one.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mara wanted to believe her. It would have been the sensible thing to believe. But she had already counted the days, and the ninth was a Thursday, and on Thursday there would be no moon at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 She bought lamp oil on the way back up the hill. She told herself that it was only because the price was good.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Chapter Three\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 By the ninth the whole village knew. They came up the path in ones and twos as the light failed, pretending that they had only come to see the view, and stood about on the wet grass with their collars turned up against the wind.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tomas came last, carrying a flask of tea and a blanket, and said nothing at all, which was the kindest thing he had done in years.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At a quarter to nine Mara climbed the one hundred and forty-two steps, trimmed the wick the way she had been taught as a child, and struck a match.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The beam went out across the black water. For a long moment there was nothing. Then, far out beyond the bar, a second light answered.\par}
}
